  "body": "## What was wrong\n\n4.33.0 replaced the full-scan percentile with a streamed cursor that stops once the highest needed rank has gone past. Measured properly, that is **1.5x (unfiltered) to 1.74x (provider-filtered)** the cost of the plain `fetchall()` it replaced — on identical results:\n\n```\nunfiltered                     streaming 265.3 ms   fetchall 175.2 ms   -> 1.51x\nprovider filter (~12.5k rows)  streaming 123.1 ms   fetchall  70.7 ms   -> 1.74x\n```\n\nTwo reasons, both knowable in advance:\n\n- **`p95` needs a rank near the end of the row count whatever the filter is**, so \"stop early\" has almost nothing to stop early from — the saving is on the last 5%.\n- **`fetchall()` is one bulk C-level fetch** inside the `sqlite3` module; iterating the cursor pays a Python-level `__next__` per row. At 12.5k rows that overhead is the entire difference. The query plan is identical either way — only the Python-side consumption changed.\n\n## The fix\n\n`_percentiles` is now the same single query and single fetch the `_percentile` helper used, with p50 and p95 sharing one sorted list instead of two module-level calls. **It is not faster than 4.32.0**, and the docstring now says so along with why the two cleverer mechanisms lost, so nobody re-attempts them.\n\nThe rest of 4.33.0 stands and is where the actual wins are: the LRU-bounded stats cache, the capped breakdowns, and `pulse()` — the one that matters, because it stops the expensive query running at all when nothing changed.\n\n## How this shipped, since the process failure is the interesting part\n\nThe end-to-end A/B I gated the release on **could not see it**. `stats()` takes 700–1400 ms on this machine with run-to-run swings of 2–4x; the regression is ~90 ms. It sat under the noise floor, and three alternating rounds showed the branch level-or-faster.\n\nWhat found it was measuring **the disputed function alone, both implementations in one process, alternating order, asserting both return identical results first**. That comparison is unambiguous in 15 reps.\n\nThe whole-call benchmark is still necessary — it is what caught the `duration_ms` index that made `stats()` 2.2x slower by poisoning other queries' plans, which no microbenchmark would ever show. Neither replaces the other.\n\n**Credit:** this was reported by the subagent that wrote the original change, after I had already merged and released it. I had dismissed the same concern on my own noisier numbers.\n\n## Verification\n\n```\n$ uv run pytest tests/core/test_request_log.py tests/api/test_admin_requests.py -q\n58 passed in 44.75s\n```\n\nIncludes the pinned test asserting exact p50/p95 values against hand-computed expectations on both filtered and unfiltered inputs.\n\nRe-measured after the fix, same harness:\n\n```\nunfiltered                     0.95x the reference implementation\nprovider filter (~12.5k rows)  1.02x\n```\n\ni.e. the same code path, as expected. `ruff format`, `ruff check`, `ty check` clean.\n\n## Residual risks\n\nNone new. This restores 4.32.0's percentile behaviour and cost exactly; the surrounding 4.33.0 changes are untouched.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)",
