    "message": "fix(analytics): a percentile that stops early is slower than one that does not\n\n4.33.0 replaced the full-scan percentile with a streamed cursor that stops\nonce the highest needed rank has gone past. Measured properly, that is 1.5x\n(unfiltered) to 1.74x (provider-filtered) the cost of the plain fetchall it\nreplaced, on identical results:\n\n    unfiltered                     streaming 265.3 ms   fetchall 175.2 ms\n    provider filter (~12.5k rows)  streaming 123.1 ms   fetchall  70.7 ms\n\nTwo reasons, and both were knowable in advance:\n\n- p95 needs a rank near the end of the row count whatever the filter is, so\n  \"stop early\" has almost nothing to stop early from. The saving is on the\n  last 5%.\n- fetchall() is one bulk C-level fetch inside the sqlite3 module; iterating\n  the cursor pays a Python-level __next__ per row. At 12.5k rows that\n  overhead is the whole difference. The query plan is identical either way --\n  only the Python-side consumption changed.\n\nSo _percentiles is now the same single query and single fetch as the\n_percentile helper it replaced, with p50 and p95 sharing one sorted list\ninstead of two module-level calls. It is not faster than 4.32.0 and the\ndocstring now says so, along with why the two cleverer mechanisms lost, so\nnobody re-attempts them.\n\nThe rest of 4.33.0 stands and is where the actual wins are: the LRU-bounded\nstats cache, the capped breakdowns, and pulse() -- which is the one that\nmatters, because it stops the expensive query running at all when nothing\nchanged.\n\nHow this got shipped, since the process failure is the interesting part: the\nend-to-end A/B I gated on could not see it. stats() takes 700-1400 ms on this\nmachine with run-to-run swings of 2-4x, and the regression is ~90 ms. It sat\nunder the noise floor, and three alternating rounds showed the branch\nlevel-or-faster. What found it was measuring the disputed function alone,\nboth implementations in one process, alternating order, asserting both return\nidentical results first. That comparison is unambiguous in 15 reps.\n\nThe whole-call benchmark is still necessary -- it is what caught the index\nthat made stats() 2.2x slower by poisoning other queries' plans, which no\nmicrobenchmark would ever show. Neither replaces the other.\n\nCredit: this was reported by the subagent that wrote the original change,\nafter I had already merged and released it. I had dismissed the same concern\non my own noisier numbers.\n\nVerification: 58 tests pass, including the pinned test asserting exact p50/p95\nvalues against hand-computed expectations. Re-measured after the fix: 0.95x\nand 1.02x against the reference implementation, i.e. the same code path.\nruff, ty clean.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>",
